# Python Port Plan — Step 12: Context Management

## Goal

Port `week1_baseline/ruby/12_context` to `week1_baseline/python/12_context`.
This is a large step: it adds real context-window tracking, colour-coded
usage indicators, and automatic/manual compaction; it adds "reasoning"
(extended-thinking) content-block support across all 5 LLM backends
(Anthropic, OpenAI, Gemini, Ollama, OllamaCloud) — including OpenAI's switch
from the Chat Completions API to the Responses API entirely; and it **removes
the `Tasks::Base`/`Tasks::Player` abstraction layer entirely**, inlining what
it did directly into `Config`. Version bumps to `0.12.0`.

Confirmed via diff against `11_tui`: `agent.rb`, `config.rb`, `context.rb`,
`errors.rb` (reformatted only, same 4 classes), `logger.rb`,
`prompt_builder.rb` (unchanged — pure facade, no diff), `repl.rb`, `tui.rb`,
`version.rb`, `tools/file_system.rb`, `boukensha.rb`, and all 5
`backends/*.rb` changed. New file: `lib/boukensha/models.rb`. **Removed**:
`lib/boukensha/tasks/base.rb`, `lib/boukensha/tasks/player.rb`,
`prompts/system.md` (the bundled default system prompt). `client.rb`,
`message.rb`, `registry.rb`, `run_dsl.rb`, `tool.rb`, `tools/mud.rb`,
`tools/shell.rb`, `boukensha_loader.rb`, `examples/example.rb` are all
byte-identical to step 11 and need no new translation work.

## Design decisions confirmed with user

- **`Models.context_window` vs. each backend's own `MODELS` table — reproduce
  faithfully.** Ruby's new `Boukensha::Models.context_window(model)` (used to
  default `Context#context_window` in `run`/`repl` when no explicit
  `context_window:` is passed) is a *separate*, much cruder table than each
  backend's own `MODELS` constant: it only has 3 Anthropic model entries (all
  hard-coded to 200,000, even though Anthropic's own `MODELS` table says
  Opus/Sonnet are 1,000,000-token windows) and a `32,000` default for
  everything else (so OpenAI/Gemini/Ollama models — which really have
  400K–1M+ windows per their own tables — silently get treated as 32,000
  unless the caller passes `context_window:` explicitly). The two tables are
  never reconciled in Ruby. **Confirmed: port this exactly as-is**, including
  the mismatch — this is consistent with this project's standing precedent
  of reproducing Ruby's real behavior (bugs and reversions included) rather
  than silently fixing it. Document the discrepancy clearly in the Python
  README.

