## Porting notes / gotchas to handle deliberately

1. **`Context.system` is required, `Context.task` is gone.** Python's
   `Context.__init__` signature becomes
   `def __init__(self, *, system: str | None, context_window: int = 200_000, working_dir: str | None = None, compaction_threshold: float = 0.85) -> None:`
   — no `task` parameter at all (drop `self.task` and the `task.task_name()`
   branch from `__str__`). `system` has no default value in Ruby (a required
   keyword) — mirror with no `= None` default on the Python parameter either
   (still typed `str | None` since the *value* can be null, just not
   omittable).
2. **`needs_compaction`'s default-from-instance-attribute trick.** Ruby's
   `def needs_compaction?(threshold: compaction_threshold)` uses the
   instance's own `compaction_threshold` attribute as the keyword default —
   Python can't reference `self` in a parameter default, so:
   `def needs_compaction(self, threshold: float | None = None) -> bool: threshold = self.compaction_threshold if threshold is None else threshold; return self.usage_fraction >= threshold`.
3. **`compact_messages!`'s drop-count arithmetic**: `drop_count = min(ceil(len(messages) * 0.40), len(messages) - 2)`, then
   `drop_count = max(drop_count, 0)` — port with `math.ceil`, preserving the
   exact `0.40`/`- 2`/`max(…, 0)` sequence (edge case: 0 or 1 messages must
   not go negative or attempt to drop more than exist).
